---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArrowIcon from '../components/ArrowIcon.astro';
import SectionEyebrow from '../components/SectionEyebrow.astro';
import '../styles/philosophy.css';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Our Philosophy"
  navCurrent="philosophy"
  description="Children learn best when they're guided and when they're trusted to lead. Our philosophy: play-based, inquiry-led learning with intentional structure, building three things in every child."
>

  {/* ── Page header ─────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="page-header-inner">
      <SectionEyebrow number="00" label="Our Philosophy" />
      <h1 class="display">
        Two ways of learning.<br />
        <em>Three things</em> every child takes with them.
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Children learn best when they are guided — <em>and</em> when they are
        trusted to lead. That simple belief shapes everything we do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  {/* ── 01 · The two ways ───────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <SectionEyebrow number="01" label="How we teach" />
      <h2 class="display">
        Some of the day is <em>structured</em>. The rest is <em>theirs</em>.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <p>
          Some of a child's day here is <strong>structured</strong> — early
          literacy and numbers, music, movement, the small daily routines that
          build focus and confidence.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rest is <strong>child-led exploration</strong> — open-ended time
          where children follow their own curiosity, and we follow with them.
        </p>
        <div class="pull-quote">
          <p>School-ready. Life-ready.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          We lead with exploration on purpose. Structure has clear strengths,
          and we use it deliberately. But the qualities that stay with a child
          for life — <strong>curiosity, resilience, imagination, the ability to
          get along with others</strong> — grow most when children have the
          freedom to wonder, try, fail, and try again.
        </p>
        <p>
          So we build the day around play and inquiry, and bring structure in
          where it genuinely helps.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 02 · The three things ───────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="minds-band">
    <div class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <SectionEyebrow number="02" label="What every child takes with them" />
        <h2 class="display">Three things, built <em>every day</em>.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="minds-list">
        <article class="mind-block">
          <span class="mind-roman">i.</span>
          <h3>Courage</h3>
          <p class="mind-promise">The nerve to keep going — and to be <em>seen</em>.</p>
          <p>
            When a tower of blocks falls for the third time, we don't rush in to
            rebuild it. We stay close, and we let the child discover that they
            can try again. And when a child stands at the edge of being seen —
            hand half-raised, or about to show what they made — we make that feel
            safe too. Resilience isn't built by removing the struggle; courage
            isn't built by removing the risk of being seen. Both are built by
            being supported through them.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="mind-block">
          <span class="mind-roman">ii.</span>
          <h3>Curiosity</h3>
          <p class="mind-promise">Knowing <em>how</em> to learn — and wanting to.</p>
          <p>
            We care less about a child having the right answer and more about
            what they do when they don't have one yet. Asking a question, making
            a guess, testing it, being wrong, and trying another way — that's
            the muscle we strengthen every day. It outlasts any single fact.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="mind-block">
          <span class="mind-roman">iii.</span>
          <h3>Belonging</h3>
          <p class="mind-promise">At home with <em>yourself</em>, and with others.</p>
          <p>
            A child who can say "I'm frustrated" instead of grabbing, or who
            notices a friend left out and brings them in, is learning something
            as important as any letter or number. And a child who feels at home
            in more than one world — proud of where they come from and wholly at
            home here — carries a steadiness that lasts. We grow both: the warmth
            to be with others, and the security of knowing who you are.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 03 · What we believe ────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <SectionEyebrow number="03" label="What we believe" />
      <h2 class="display">A few convictions, <em>said plainly</em>.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-narrow" style="margin-bottom: 60px;">
      <p>
        These are the beliefs the rest of it rests on. We say them out loud
        because the families they fit tend to recognise them straight away.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="beliefs">
      <article class="belief">
        <h3>Children are more capable than we expect.</h3>
        <p>
          So we let them try the hard thing, and step in less than you might
          think. Confidence is built on the far side of a struggle — not
          instead of it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="belief">
        <h3>Play is how young children learn best.</h3>
        <p>
          The block tower, the mud kitchen, the made-up game — that isn't a
          break from the learning. It <em>is</em> the learning.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="belief">
        <h3>Structure should serve the child.</h3>
        <p>
          We use it where it builds focus and calm, and not for its own sake.
          Rhythm, never rigidity.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="belief">
        <h3>Outdoors is a classroom.</h3>
        <p>
          Weather, dirt, climbing, and the small risks that come with them are
          core to the day — not recess between the real lessons.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="belief">
        <h3>A child can belong here without leaving anything behind.</h3>
        <p>
          Home languages and heritage stay in the room. Wholly at home here;
          wholly proud of where they come from.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="belief">
        <h3>We prepare children for life, not just for school.</h3>
        <p>
          School readiness comes — but resilience, curiosity, and how a child
          treats others matter every bit as much.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="belief">
        <h3>We praise the effort, not the talent.</h3>
        <p>
          We notice the trying. No child here is "just not a maths person" at
          four years old.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 04 · Life, not just school ──────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <SectionEyebrow number="04" label="Why it matters" />
      <h2 class="display">
        Preparing children for <em>life</em>, not just for school.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-narrow">
      <p>
        The world our children will grow into is changing faster than any
        curriculum can predict. As more of what we do becomes automated, the
        abilities that stay valuable are the deeply human ones — creativity,
        empathy, judgment, and the confidence to navigate the unknown.
      </p>
      <p>
        School readiness matters, and our children are well prepared for it. But
        we're aiming further than that: we want children who are ready for
        <em>life</em>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 04 · Closing + CTA ──────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="final-cta">
    <h2 class="display">A day here looks <em>calm and purposeful</em>.</h2>
    <p>
      Structure gives the day its rhythm; exploration gives it its spark. Your
      child will be known, challenged at the right level, and free to follow the
      questions that light them up.
    </p>
    <a href="/book-a-tour" class="btn-primary">
      Come see it for yourself
      <ArrowIcon />
    </a>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
